WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.430
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:10.309
The goal of this work is to have a robot that
automatically creates a 3D model of an indoor

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:14.299
space, including path planning and controlling
attention.

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:17.580
Now this immediately sounds like quite a challenging
task.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:24.550
This robot uses an RGBD camera, so beyond
the colors, it also gets some depth information

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.870
that describes how far the viewed objects
are from the observer.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:33.510
From this information, it tries to create
a 3D digital model of these interiors.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:39.790
It not only does that, but it also constantly
replans its trajectory based on newly found

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:40.790
areas.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:45.590
These paths have to be smooth and walkable
without bumping into objects, and of course,

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.760
adapt to the topology of the building.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.010
You'll see in a moment why even this mundane
sounding smooth path part is really challenging

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.040
to accomplish.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.050
Spoiler alert: it's about singularities.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:01.780
With this proposed technique, the robot takes
a lap in the building and builds a rough representation

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.600
of it, kind of like a minimap in your favorite
computer games.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.810
Previous techniques worked with potential
and gradient fields to guide the navigation.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:15.460
The issue with these is that the green dots
that you see here represent singularities.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.950
These are degenerate points that introduce
ambiguity to the path planning process and

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.670
reduce the efficiency of the navigation.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:27.369
The red dots are sinks, which are even worse,
because these can trap the robot.

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:32.450
This new proposed tensor field representation
contains a lot fewer singularities, and its

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.229
favorable mathematical properties make it
sink-free.

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:40.270
This leads to much better path planning which
is crucial for maintaining high reconstruction

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.270
quality.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:45.700
If you have a look at the paper, you'll see
several occurrences of the word "advection".

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.520
This is particularly cool because these robot
paths are planned in these gradient or tensor

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.459
fields represent the vortices and flow directions
similarly to how fluid flows are computed

00:01:57.459 --> 00:01:58.749
in simulations.

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:01.200
Many of which you have seen in this series.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.919
Beautiful, love it.

00:02:02.919 --> 00:02:07.570
However, as advection can't guarantee that
we'll have a full coverage of the building,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.980
this proposed technique borrows classic structures
from graph theory.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.720
Graph theory is used to model the connections
between railway stations or to represent people

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.409
and their relationships in a social network.

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:25.180
And here, a method to construct a minimum
spanning tree was borrowed to help deciding

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:30.459
which direction to take at intersections for
optimal coverage with miminal effort.

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:36.799
Robots, fluids, graph theory, some of my favorite
topics of all time, so you probably know how

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:42.560
happy I was to see all these theories come
together to create something really practical.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.060
This is an amazing paper, make sure to have
a look at it, it is available in the video

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.060
description.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.560
The source code of this project is also available.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.409
If you have enjoyed this episode, make sure
to subscribe to the series and click the bell

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.290
icon to never miss an episode.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.120
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

